# Type erasure — what the JVM sees

## Generics vanish at run time

```java
List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();      // what you write
String s = list.get(0);

List list = new ArrayList();                 // what runs — erased
String s = (String) list.get(0);             // cast inserted for you
```

`<T>` → its bound (or `Object`); type info verifies, then is thrown away.

## The one consequence

```java
new ArrayList<String>().getClass() == new ArrayList<Integer>().getClass();  // true
```

**All instantiations share one class** — the run time doesn't know the argument.

## So you can't, at run time

- No **`T.class`** / `List<String>.class` (raw `List.class` ok)
- No **`instanceof List<String>`** (only `List<?>`)
- No **`new T()`** / **`new T[]`** — pass a `Supplier<T>` / `Class<T>`
- No overloads differing only by argument (`f(List<String>)` vs `f(List<Integer>)`)

## Why: **backward compatibility** with pre-Java-5 raw code.
